18. View validation reports.
19. View failed generations.
20. Roll back to a previous approved policy version.

Only authorized Super Admin accounts may modify global textbook generation policies.

Other administrators may have read-only access or explicitly delegated permissions.

No individual school administrator may weaken, replace or override centrally mandated academic safeguards.

1.3 Configuration granularity

Support three policy levels:

A. GLOBAL POLICY

Rules that apply to every textbook regardless of subject or class.

Examples:

- Academic accuracy.
- Curriculum grounding.
- Visual-verbal consistency.
- No fabricated curriculum requirements.
- Mandatory validation.
- Publication controls.

B. CLASS-LEVEL POLICY

Rules that apply to a particular educational level and class.

Examples:

- Vocabulary complexity.
- Reading difficulty.
- Sentence length.
- Conceptual depth.
- Mathematical sophistication.
- Expected prior knowledge.
- Appropriate instructional progression.

C. SUBJECT-AND-CLASS POLICY

Rules specifically configured for a subject within a particular class.

Examples:

- Primary 2 Mathematics.
- Primary 5 English.
- JSS 2 Basic Science.
- SSS 2 Chemistry.

The most specific policy must be resolved for every generation request.

Specific policies may strengthen global requirements but must never weaken mandatory global safeguards.

Where a subject-class policy is missing, the system may inherit valid class-level and global policies only if those policies provide all required controls.

Otherwise, generation must be blocked pending Super Admin configuration.

