PART 5: INDEPENDENT TEXTBOOK QUALITY ASSURANCE

Implement a separate quality assurance process.

The quality assurance engine must inspect the generated textbook independently of the generation operation.

Use deterministic programmatic checks wherever possible and AI-assisted academic and multimodal evaluation where necessary.

5.1 Quality scorecard

Implement the following initial configurable scoring system:

Quality dimension| Weight
NERDC curriculum alignment| 25%
Academic accuracy| 20%
Class-level appropriateness| 15%
Content completeness| 15%
Graphical and verbal consistency| 10%
Pedagogical quality| 10%
Assessment alignment| 5%
Total| 100%

5.2 Quality decisions

- Below 80%: REJECT.
- 80–89%: CORRECTION REQUIRED.
- 90–94%: ELIGIBLE FOR ACADEMIC REVIEW.
- 95–100%: ELIGIBLE FOR ACADEMIC REVIEW AND APPROVAL.

A high numerical score must never automatically constitute academic approval.

5.3 Critical failure conditions

Regardless of the numerical score, reject a textbook containing:

- Missing mandatory curriculum objectives.
- Fabricated curriculum references.
- Materially incorrect academic explanations.
- Incorrect mathematical answers.
- Materially misleading educational diagrams.
- Critical graphical-verbal contradictions.
- Invalid answer keys.
- Missing mandatory textbook sections.

Persist detailed validation reports and evidence.

Support section-level correction and regeneration.

Do not regenerate an entire textbook unnecessarily when a specific chapter or illustration can be corrected independently.

